
               In The Beginning, There Was HARBRAD ...
                    Copyright (c) 1991 Lee Bradley

It might be instructive to discuss a few HARBRAD examples. The simplest
one is EXAMPLE.HBS. This program displays the numbers 10, 20, 30, ... ,
900 on the console. Not a terribly useful program, but it does
illustrate quite a few important things.

Here's the code:

     00,+000   
     01,+000                EXAMPLE.HBS
     02,+000   
     03,+113      L    TN   START AT 10
     04,+800   LB PT        PRINT THE VALUE
     05,+313      A    TN   BUMP THE VALUE BY 10
     06,+412      S    NT   TAKE AWAY 910
     07,-311      BRZ  BY   GET OUT IF DONE
     08,+312      A    NT   ADD IT BACK IF NOT DONE
     09,+000                 
     10,-104      BR   LB   OTHERWISE KEEP ON TRUCKIN'
     11,+900   BY STOP      ALL DONE
     12,+910   NT DW   +910 UPPER LIMIT +10
     13,+010   TN DW   +010 THE INITIAL VALUE & INCREMENT
     14,+000   

This program outputs (see the code at machine location 04 above) what's
in the Accumulator. All computers have such a thing. The 8080 CPU has
not only an "Accumulator" (register A,) but it also has other registers
(registers F, B, C, D, E, H, L.)

The Accumulator is an *implied* operand in *every* HARBRAD instruction
(except STOP.) That is, the instruction at machine location 03 Loads the
Accumulator with the data that is at machine location 13. The
instruction at location 07 will branch (jump) to location 11 *if* the
Accumulator is zero.

You'll notice there are a few gaps in the program above. Location 09 for
example. Its presence has an effect, but not one that you feel when you
run the program. These are sometimes referred to as NOP's, (No
OPerations.) They take up room, and cause assemblers to assign labeled
locations different values than they would if they were not there. In
fact, let's see what it would look like if they were not there. We
really have 5 such lines, one of which simply documents the name of the
program, EXAMPLE.HBS.  Let's keep just that one and delete the other 4.

To do this, run the editor, ZDE, on EXAMPLE.HBS, and delete the four
"NOP" lines. You should get:

                  EXAMPLE.HBS
        L    TN   START AT 10
     LB PT        PRINT THE VALUE
        A    TN   BUMP THE VALUE BY 10
        S    NT   TAKE AWAY 910
        BRZ  BY   GET OUT IF DONE
        A    OT   ADD IT BACK IF NOT DONE
        BR   LB   OTHERWISE KEEP ON TRUCKIN'
     BY STOP      ALL DONE
     NT DW   +910 UPPER LIMIT +10
     TN DW   +010 THE INITIAL VALUE & INCREMENT
        END       EXAMPLE

To run this program, you must "assemble" it. This can be done manually
or automatically. Running the assembler, HASM, we get:

What we get is a bug. The assembler is not very smart and fails to
report things like unreferenced labels and unlabeled references. If you
look carefully, you will see that

        A    OT   ADD IT BACK IF NOT DONE

uses an "unlabeled reference" (ie. OT is undefined.) So perhaps our
first lesson in debugging is:

When something does not work, try to figure out who's (what's) at fault.

It took me about 5 minutes to discover this undefined symbol bug. The
first thing I noticed was that the assembler threw some garbage at the
screen when I ran it. So I went back in with the editor to see what
might be wrong. Also, when I ran the buggy program, the emulator gave me
a Subscript out of Bounds error message, early on. Eventually I spotted
the problem. I changed the OT to an NT, re-assembled and ran. It worked.

It turns out, the reason I had an OT in the first place is this: I used
to have the value +110 at the place which used to be named OT (note the
mnemonic choice of label; One hundred and Ten = OT.) Then I decided to
change it to +910. Thus the label NT (Nine hundred and Ten) was born. I
just failed to change ALL occurences of OT to NT. Bugs are born by
changes like this.

The next step is to *generalize*. Let's change EXAMPLE.HBS into
BEGIN.HBS.  It will *ReaD* three values. The beginning number, the
increment value and the ending number. Here it is:

                  BEGIN.HBS
        RD
        ST   SN   START AT SN
        RD
        ST   IN   INCREMENT WITH IN
        RD
        ST   EN   END AT EN
        L    SN
     LB PT        PRINT THE VALUE
        A    IN   BUMP THE VALUE BY IN
        S    EN   TAKE AWAY EN+IN
        BRP  BY   GET OUT IF DONE
        A    EN   ADD IT BACK IF NOT DONE
        BR   LB   AND KEEP ON TRUCKIN'
     BY STOP      ALL DONE
     SN DS   01   THE INITIAL VALUE
     IN DS   01   THE INCREMENT
     EN DS   01   THE END VALUE
        END       EXAMPLE

Note how there are no more DW's (Define Word.) They have been replaced
by some DS's (Define Storage.) We only need one (01) "word" for each of
the three numbers.

Assemble this one with HASM. Run it with HRUN.

     00,+000               BEGIN.HBS
     01,+700     RD
     02,+215     ST   SN   START AT SN
     03,+700     RD
     04,+216     ST   IN   INCREMENT WITH IN
     05,+700     RD
     06,+217     ST   EN   END AT EN
     07,+115     L    SN
     08,+800  LB PT        PRINT THE VALUE
     09,+316     A    IN   BUMP THE VALUE BY IN
     10,+417     S    EN   TAKE AWAY EN+IN
     11,-214     BRP  BY   GET OUT IF DONE
     12,+317     A    EN   ADD IT BACK IF NOT DONE
     13,-108     BR   LB   AND KEEP ON TRUCKIN'
     14,+900  BY STOP      ALL DONE
     15,+000  SN DS   01   THE INITIAL VALUE
     16,+000  IN DS   01   THE INCREMENT
     17,+000  EN DS   01   THEN END VALUE

There are several other example programs on the disk. PRIMES computes
prime numbers.

     00,+000                PRIMES.HBS 11/18/91
     01,+700     RD         GET UPPER LIMIT.
     02,+256     ST   N1    MUST NOT BE TOO BIG!
     03,+151     L    ON
     04,+252     ST   J1    J <- 1
     05,+351     A    ON
     06,+257     ST   A1    A(1) <- 2
     07,+800     PT         PRINT 2, FIRST
     08,+351     A    ON    PRIME.
     09,+254     ST   I1    I <- 3
     10,+151  IL L    ON    I LOOP
     11,+253     ST   K1    K <- 1
     12,+148  KL L    LA    K LOOP.  LOAD
     13,+353     A    K1    LOAD A.
     14,+451     S    ON
     15,+216     ST   L1
     16,+000  L1 DS   01    AC  <- A(K)
     17,+255     ST   SV    SAVE IT.
     18,+154     L    I1
     19,+655     D    SV
     20,+555     M    SV
     21,+654     D    I1
     22,-242     BRP  BI    IF I/A(K)=
     23,+155     L    SV    INT(I/A(K))
     24,+555     M    SV    THEN BUMP I.
     25,+454     S    I1
     26,-232     BRP  BJ    IF A(K) > SQR(I)
     27,+153     L    K1    THEN BUMP J.
     28,+351     A    ON
     29,+253     ST   K1    K <- K+1
     30,+452     S    J1
     31,-512     BRNP KL
     32,+152  BJ L    J1    BUMP J.
     33,+351     A    ON
     34,+252     ST   J1    J <- J+1
     35,+149     L    SA    LOAD STORE A.
     36,+352     A    J1
     37,+451     S    ON
     38,+241     ST   L2
     39,+154     L    I1    AC  <- I
     40,+800     PT         PRINT I, PRIME.
     41,+000  L2 DS   01    A(J) <- I
     42,+154  BI L    I1    BUMP I BY 2.
     43,+350     A    TW
     44,+254     ST   I1    I <- I + 2
     45,+456     S    N1
     46,-510     BRNP IL
     47,+900     STOP       STOP
     48,+157  LA L    A1    LOAD A
     49,+257  SA ST   A1    STORE A
     50,+002  TW DW   +002  THE VALUE 2.
     51,+001  ON DW   +001  THE VALUE 1.
     52,+000  J1 DS   01    J
     53,+000  K1 DS   01    K
     54,+000  I1 DS   01    I
     55,+000  SV DS   01    SAVE AREA
     56,+000  N1 DS   01    UPPER LIMIT.
     57,+000  A1 DS   01    ARRAY A STARTS HERE

This is (obviously) a much more complicated program. It figures out and
prints the first 44 prime numbers. It will not be explained here.  But
it will be pointed out that in order to pull this feat off in 100 words
of code, it was necessary to let the instructions modify themselves!

Such a practice (self-modifying code) is generally frowned upon. But
occasionally, it's the only way you can do it.

Another equally useless and almost as interesting program is ADDDEC. 
This program adds two 10-digit decimal numbers and prints their sum.

         2352828491
         9393929292
     +  -----------
        11746757783

This is also a non-trivial program, given the very limited resources of
HARBRAD.

Both this program and PRIMES use just about every "word" of the HARBRAD
computer's memory (all 100 locations.)

When ADDDEC is run, you see 20 ? marks, and you enter the 20 decimal
digits. The first 10 are for the first number (high order down to 1's
place) and the second 10 are for the second number. The 11-digit sum is
computed and displayed. Think about how *you* add two 10-digit decimal
numbers. That's what the program does. It, like PRIMES, is
self-modifying.

These last two examples would appeal to the "hacker," the person who
likes a complicated task and who is into such things as self-modifying
code. Back on earth, how would you convert Centigrade temperatures to
Fahrenheit?

     00,+000                  CONVTEMP.HBS
     01,+700   TP RD          GET CENTIGRADE
     02,+507      M    NI     USE FORMULA
     03,+608      D    FI     F =
     04,+309      A    TT     9*C/5 + 32
     05,+800      PT          PRINT FAHRENHEIT
     06,+900      STOP
     07,+009   NI DW   +009   THE NUMBER 9
     08,+005   FI DW   +005   THE NUMBER 5
     09,+032   TT DW   +032   THE NUMBER 32

You run it, it asks you (with its ?) for a Centigrade temperature, you
give it one and it computes and prints its Fahrenheit equivalent.

Study at least the first and last example programs. Try to program a few
things yourself in this language. Use the assembler and the emulator. If
you can do it in HARBRAD, you are not far from being able to do it in
Z80 assembler, a MUCH more powerful language for a MUCH more powerful
computer!  And a REAL computer, at that!

Of course, read HARBRAD.DOC first. If you started with this file, you 
might think you are ready to program in HARBRAD. But there is nothing 
like reading the documentation when you need something clarified.

